1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
The gas in this camping stove is mostly

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:05,440
butane, four carbons long, and it boils near

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:06,800
minus one degree.

4
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:09,360
Candle wax is made of the same kind

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:12,960
of molecule, alkanes, but each chain has twenty

6
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,120
or more carbons, and it's a solid at

7
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,000
room temperature.

8
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:19,280
Same family, very different substances.

9
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,640
So how does carbon's bonding explain the difference?

10
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:26,986
Start with shape, and why chains zigzag.

11
00:00:27,547 --> 00:00:30,106
A carbon with four single bonds has four

12
00:00:30,187 --> 00:00:32,746
groups of bonding electrons around it, and those

13
00:00:32,827 --> 00:00:34,986
groups push each other as far apart as

14
00:00:35,067 --> 00:00:35,626
they can.

15
00:00:36,187 --> 00:00:39,226
In three dimensions, that's a tetrahedral shape, with

16
00:00:39,387 --> 00:00:41,626
about one hundred and nine point five degrees

17
00:00:41,707 --> 00:00:42,986
between the bonds.

18
00:00:43,307 --> 00:00:46,346
So alkane carbons, like those in ethane, are

19
00:00:46,467 --> 00:00:49,546
tetrahedral, and a chain of them zigzags.

20
00:00:50,767 --> 00:00:52,526
A double bond changes that.

21
00:00:52,607 --> 00:00:55,726
Each carbon in ethene has only three bonding

22
00:00:55,807 --> 00:00:58,766
directions: two to hydrogens, and one to the

23
00:00:58,847 --> 00:00:59,566
other carbon.

24
00:01:00,127 --> 00:01:02,686
The double bond counts as one direction, because

25
00:01:02,767 --> 00:01:04,526
both of its bonds point at the same

26
00:01:04,607 --> 00:01:05,246
atom.

27
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:08,286
Three directions spread out flat, about one hundred

28
00:01:08,367 --> 00:01:10,766
and twenty degrees apart, so the shape is

29
00:01:10,847 --> 00:01:11,806
trigonal planar.

30
00:01:12,287 --> 00:01:14,846
The double bond's second bond sits above and

31
00:01:14,927 --> 00:01:17,326
below the line between the carbons, and it's

32
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:18,606
weaker than the first.

33
00:01:19,834 --> 00:01:22,713
In ethyne, with a triple bond, each carbon

34
00:01:22,794 --> 00:01:25,673
has just two directions, so they point opposite

35
00:01:25,754 --> 00:01:28,433
ways: a straight line, one hundred and eighty

36
00:01:28,474 --> 00:01:29,033
degrees.

37
00:01:29,434 --> 00:01:30,313
That's linear.

38
00:01:30,954 --> 00:01:33,353
Shape belongs to each carbon, not to the

39
00:01:33,434 --> 00:01:34,233
whole molecule.

40
00:01:34,874 --> 00:01:37,193
In propene, the two carbons of the double

41
00:01:37,274 --> 00:01:39,833
bond are trigonal planar, but the C H

42
00:01:39,914 --> 00:01:42,793
three carbon on the end is still tetrahedral.

43
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:46,640
Line up the alkanes, from methane up to

44
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:47,760
octane.

45
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,920
Each one has one more C H two

46
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:51,280
than the last.

47
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:53,680
A family like that is called a homologous

48
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:56,640
series: members built the same way, with the

49
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:59,120
same functional group if they have one, each

50
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:01,280
differing by C H two.

51
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,000
One general formula gives every member.

52
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,720
For alkanes, it's C n H two n

53
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:07,920
plus two.

54
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:10,480
For octane, n is eight, so that's C

55
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:11,920
eight H eighteen.

56
00:02:13,134 --> 00:02:16,013
Alkenes form a series too, and their functional

57
00:02:16,094 --> 00:02:18,493
group is the C double bond C.

58
00:02:18,814 --> 00:02:22,173
Making one double bond takes away two hydrogens,

59
00:02:22,254 --> 00:02:25,213
so alkenes are C n H two n.

60
00:02:25,854 --> 00:02:28,333
A triple bond takes away two more, so

61
00:02:28,414 --> 00:02:31,613
alkynes are C n H two n minus

62
00:02:31,694 --> 00:02:32,013
two.

63
00:02:32,894 --> 00:02:36,253
Alkanes have no functional group, just single bonds.

64
00:02:36,894 --> 00:02:39,373
Members of a series share the same bonding,

65
00:02:39,454 --> 00:02:42,253
so they react alike, while their physical properties

66
00:02:42,414 --> 00:02:43,533
change gradually.

67
00:02:44,734 --> 00:02:47,453
Don't confuse a series with isomers.

68
00:02:47,934 --> 00:02:51,053
Propane and butane differ by C H two,

69
00:02:51,134 --> 00:02:52,893
so they're neighbours in a series.

70
00:02:53,454 --> 00:02:57,373
Butane and two-methylpropane share one formula, so they're

71
00:02:57,454 --> 00:02:58,413
isomers.

72
00:02:58,734 --> 00:03:02,093
Series members have different formulas; isomers have the

73
00:03:02,254 --> 00:03:03,213
same formula.

74
00:03:04,434 --> 00:03:05,953
Now, properties.

75
00:03:06,194 --> 00:03:09,793
Inside each molecule, strong covalent bonds hold the

76
00:03:09,874 --> 00:03:10,833
atoms together.

77
00:03:11,314 --> 00:03:13,153
That's intramolecular bonding.

78
00:03:13,634 --> 00:03:16,993
Between molecules there are much weaker attractions, called

79
00:03:17,154 --> 00:03:18,593
intermolecular forces.

80
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:23,313
Hydrocarbons are non-polar, so their only intermolecular forces

81
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:24,913
are dispersion forces.

82
00:03:25,394 --> 00:03:29,073
When a hydrocarbon boils, its molecules move apart,

83
00:03:29,194 --> 00:03:31,713
but every covalent bond stays intact.

84
00:03:32,194 --> 00:03:35,873
So boiling point measures the forces between molecules.

85
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:40,060
Dispersion forces come from electrons shifting for a

86
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:43,180
moment, which makes a temporary dipole that attracts

87
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:44,540
the next molecule.

88
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:47,420
A longer chain has more electrons, and more

89
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,900
surface to touch its neighbours, so more of

90
00:03:49,980 --> 00:03:51,860
these attractions act at once.

91
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:54,700
That takes more energy to overcome, so the

92
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:56,140
boiling point rises.

93
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:59,180
Methane boils at minus one hundred and sixty

94
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:01,820
two degrees, and octane at one hundred and

95
00:04:01,940 --> 00:04:03,180
twenty six.

96
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:05,580
Wax chains are long enough to be solid.

97
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:08,480
Branching works the other way.

98
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:13,520
Pentane, two-methylbutane and two, two-dimethylpropane are

99
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,240
all C five H twelve, with the same

100
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:19,840
number of electrons, but their boiling points fall:

101
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:23,360
thirty six, twenty eight, then ten degrees.

102
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:27,360
A straight chain, zigzagging at each tetrahedral carbon,

103
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:30,080
can lie alongside its neighbours, like strands of

104
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:31,200
uncooked spaghetti.

105
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:34,400
A branched molecule is more like a ball,

106
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:36,640
so it touches its neighbours over a smaller

107
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:37,200
area.

108
00:04:37,760 --> 00:04:40,800
Less contact means weaker dispersion forces, and a

109
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:42,080
lower boiling point.

110
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:46,460
Between the series, size still wins.

111
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:51,460
Hexane, hex-one-ene and hex-one-yne, all six carbons,

112
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:53,700
boil within about eight degrees of each other.

113
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:57,380
Decane, with ten, boils about a hundred degrees

114
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:58,140
higher.

115
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:01,060
So chain length usually matters far more than

116
00:05:01,140 --> 00:05:02,180
the type of bond.

117
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:04,680
Now solubility.

118
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,280
Water molecules hold each other with strong hydrogen

119
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:09,120
bonds.

120
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,800
To dissolve, hexane would have to push in

121
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,440
between them, breaking those bonds, and it could

122
00:05:14,520 --> 00:05:16,920
only offer weak dispersion forces back.

123
00:05:17,480 --> 00:05:20,280
So hexane stays as a separate layer, on

124
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:20,760
top.

125
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:24,760
In heptane, though, both liquids have only dispersion

126
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:26,920
forces, so they mix completely.

127
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:31,080
Like dissolves like, because similar attractions replace the

128
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:32,120
ones broken.

129
00:05:33,334 --> 00:05:37,093
So, at each carbon, count the bonding directions:

130
00:05:37,254 --> 00:05:40,773
four, three or two give tetrahedral, flat or

131
00:05:40,854 --> 00:05:41,333
straight.

132
00:05:41,894 --> 00:05:46,373
Between molecules, more contact means stronger dispersion forces,

133
00:05:46,454 --> 00:05:49,493
so longer chains boil higher, and branched chains

134
00:05:49,574 --> 00:05:50,533
boil lower.

135
00:05:51,734 --> 00:05:52,373
